92186056658 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056658 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056658 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056658 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056658 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056658 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056658 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186497829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056658 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f12506e4-c772-5058-86a7-947724feb700" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056682 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056682 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056682 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056682 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8d59fd8c-4f21-517e-93e3-05cac2fbde83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059382 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059382 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=196$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma=7.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x}=24,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram below.*\n\n![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059382 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97c8f1dc-747e-5aa4-ab4f-eb809abb560e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059382 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n = 196 \\geq 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{196}} \\\\ 24\\pm 0.98\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059383 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10039" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059383 #edb/ref 1071 "The daily percentage return of a particular stock has a population standard deviation of $\\sigma = 5\\%.$ A sample of $90$ randomly selected trading days was examined, and it was found that the mean daily return of the stock over these days was $7.5\\%.$ Find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the mean daily percentage return $\\mu$ of the entire population of trading days.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059383 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3189a42a-200e-5caa-bc76-6ac1f4ae424d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059383 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n = 90\\geq 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=7.5, \\qquad\nn = 90, \\qquad\n\\sigma = 5.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value $z_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z > z_{0.05}) = 0.05,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fc6327df99c6b88508b0c4de2e4edf6c5521974f05e2e2f02d09786937285a7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that $z_{0.05}=1.645{:}$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.050}$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.645}$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.5\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{90}} \\\\ 7.5\\pm 0.867\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059384 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059384 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=9$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma=2.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x}=-5,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram below.*\n\n![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059384 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a166c80-cc70-5dd3-9dbe-2798dc882199" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059384 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\ -5\\pm 1.097\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059823 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059823 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059823 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059823 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059824 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059824 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059824 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059825 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059825 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059825 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059825 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059825 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1045 "Confidence Intervals for One Mean: Known Population Variance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186060762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059935 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "93095345-cb92-55a9-9d0f-c4b0bb65cabb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060050 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186056658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060050 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142303" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060050 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060050 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=100$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =5.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=14,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060050 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "36cd39e3-b51f-590a-80b3-45da7f899cac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060050 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060050 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 14\\pm 0.98\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060054 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186056682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060054 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060054 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060054 #edb/ref 1071 "The weight of apples in a particular harvest is normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =12\\,\\text{g}.$ A farmer sampled $10$ apples from the harvest and found that their mean weight is $100\\,\\text{g}.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean weight $\\mu $ for the entire harvest. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060054 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d5864ffb-fe58-5217-afdf-eabb7e2ece4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060054 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060054 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=100\\,\\text{g},\\,n=10,\\,\\sigma =12\\,\\text{g}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ 100\\pm 9.775\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060762 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060762 #edb/ref 1061 37842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060762 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "55b16e12-fb99-528d-a98f-9e754fd27b3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060762 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060763 #edb/ref 1061 37861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc49103b-afb3-574a-8efd-6910f8c3b320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060764 #edb/ref 1061 37883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060764 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32d40006-ed8e-530b-8e5f-6ec28697e1f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060764 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060765 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060765 #edb/ref 1061 37884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060765 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "043270f2-b372-59f5-98a2-64919b13fa7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060765 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060766 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060766 #edb/ref 1061 37890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060766 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5f0968c6-9ace-5735-9d92-61ff226b832e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060766 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060767 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060767 #edb/ref 1061 37913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060767 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3461449b-03e6-5f5d-aff8-7cec85835701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060767 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060768 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060768 #edb/ref 1061 38032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060768 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "afbe0be3-1d3c-5408-9cda-36c61677f0fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060768 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060769 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060769 #edb/ref 1061 38686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060769 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e659ed87-b26a-54f6-bb76-e98cf99a6b01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060769 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060964 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n=10$ from a normal population with the unknown population mean $\\mu$ and *known* population variance $\\sigma^2=9.$ After processing our results, we compute the mean of this sample using the usual methods and get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=12\n$$\n\n \n\nThis is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$ However, it is unsatisfactory because we have no information regarding its reliability.\n\nFor this reason, it's often more helpful to give a range of possible values for $\\mu.$ In other words, instead of reporting a single estimate $\\overline{x},$ we might instead report a so-called **confidence interval,** which is an interval of the form\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-E, \\: \\overline{x}+E)\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is our estimate of the population mean, and\n\n- $E$ is some **margin of error**.\n\nIn addition, we need to give some indication regarding the reliability of our confidence interval. With this in mind, we will construct a $\\boldsymbol{95\\%}$ **confidence interval** for the population mean $\\mu$. The precise meaning of this will become clear shortly." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060964 #edb/ref 1065 6232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060964 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f8ebcc7b-ddba-56d2-bb41-07916f3c2d09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060964 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060965 #edb/ref 1064 "Up to now, we've assumed that the underlying population is normally distributed. However, if we have a *sufficiently large* sample, then according to the central limit theorem, we can use the approximation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\approx N(0,1).\n$$\n\nSo, even though we might not know the underlying distribution, we can still use our formula for the confidence interval of the population mean $\\mu{:}$\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060965 #edb/ref 1065 6245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060965 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4b9c6c26-d589-5847-a384-c431ad44a125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060965 #edb/ref 1067 "Large Samples" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060966 #edb/ref 1064 "It's important to be able to interpret confidence intervals correctly.\n\nImagine we conduct several independent random samples from a population. The mean of the sample will be different for each sample. Some values of $\\overline{x}$ might lie close to the population mean $\\mu,$ and some could lie further away.\n\nSuppose we construct a $90\\%$ confidence interval for each sample and plot them on the same diagram. We might get a picture similar to the one below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc8dd707751cd9e1cb0da1bf618b6d7aed740fab875d8d49c7c89fc09b77321e.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, we see that nine out of ten of our confidence intervals contain $\\mu,$ while one of them (the red one) does *not* contain the population mean $\\mu.$ \n\nThe idea is that if we construct many $90\\%$ confidence intervals, one for each sample, then approximately $90\\%$ of these confidence intervals will contain the population mean $\\mu.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060966 #edb/ref 1065 6247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060966 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d71a924b-5f1e-5a89-a56b-d540a4a53d74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060966 #edb/ref 1067 "Interpreting Confidence Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060967 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population with the unknown population mean $\\mu$ and *known* population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$ \n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are called **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the standard error of the mean\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/58/581fc2da88e4419e78389a1b5eedb127f590641d0b7ee21c489ed85184795b13.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}, & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060967 #edb/ref 1065 6275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060967 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cdae1125-df9d-525c-823d-64f129c3ab6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060967 #edb/ref 1067 "Summarizing Confidence Intervals for Normal Populations With Known Population Variance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060968 #edb/ref 1064 "In our example, the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is also normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\left(\\mu,\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nBy transforming $\\overline{X}$ to a standard normal random variable, we have\n\n$$\nZ = \\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}\\sim N(0,1).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find a $z$-interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $Z$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/50/50a0f49fe5c96ba3259832fbf1a0abcedffb342301ca069bb7b88d157a181019.png)\n\n  \n\nTo help with the notation, we define a new parameter $\\alpha.$ Since we're computing a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ is precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as $\\pm z_{\\alpha/2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/3c/3cdfd2c72bf7fe65e5d1b584d46d4c846e74615e54c739a6d1ea990c95011a8a.png)\n\n  \n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{0.025}) = P(Z < -z_{0.025}) = 0.025.\n$$\n\n  \n\nUsing a percentage points table for the standard normal distribution, we find that $z_{0.025} \\approx 1.96.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = P(Z < -1.96) = 0.025.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.96<Z<1.96)=0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, there is a $95\\%$ probability that our random variable $Z$ will lie in the interval $(-1.96, 1.96).$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We solve the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the population mean $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1.96< & \\,Z<1.96 \\\\ -1.96< & \\,\\frac{\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}<1.96 \\\\ -1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}< & \\,\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu <1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ -\\overset{―}{x}-1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}< & \\,-\\mu \\,<-\\overset{―}{x}+1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}+1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}> & \\,\\mu >\\overset{―}{x}-1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}-1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}< & \\,\\mu <\\overset{―}{x}+1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - 1.96\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\\: \\overline{x} + 1.96\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n$$\nn=10, \\qquad\n\\overline{x}=12, \\qquad\n\\sigma=3,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that our $95\\%$ confidence interval for $\\mu$ is \n\n$$\n\\big( 12- 1.859, \\: 12 + 1.859 \\big).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that we've seen how to construct a particular confidence interval, let's discuss the more general procedure." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060968 #edb/ref 1065 6398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060968 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "609f60a5-fbfd-5385-89e2-f80dd6281f39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060968 #edb/ref 1067 "Constructing Confidence Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497799 #edb/ref 1080 "14.4\\pm 2.576" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497799 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17